--TEST--
`Core\Session::start` refuses a tree that configured no store, naming the block to write rather than choosing one for it -- `rule:core-api/session-roster` and `rule:http-server/session-backend-is-shared-or-db-and-local-is-refused-at-boot`
--GET--
under=a-request
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The case answers a request, because that is the first thing `start` asks about
// (`rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`) and a program answering
// none never reaches the store question this case is about.
//
// `rule:http-server/session-backend-is-shared-or-db-and-local-is-refused-at-boot`: an absent `[session]` block is not a default backend. The safe
// answer for a store nobody chose is no store — the direction `rule:http-server/an-unsafe-or-unbounded-default-is-a-defect` takes
// for everything it leaves unconfigured — so a bare `start()` here is a refusal
// and not a session on whichever store happened to be reachable.
uint $refused = 0;
string $said = "";
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (RuntimeError $unconfigured) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    $said = $unconfigured->message;
}
echo "a bare start was refused ", $refused, " of 1\n";

// The refusal is only worth something if it says what to write. An operator
// reading "no session store is configured" and nothing else has to go and find
// the directive's name, which is the failure a message that names it removes.
if (Core\Str::contains($said, "[session] backend")) {
    echo "and it names the block to write\n";
} else {
    echo "and it names nothing: ", $said, "\n";
}

// It is a `RuntimeError`, so it is catchable by the root of spec § 10's tree as
// well: a program that cannot start a session is one that can say so, not one
// that dies. The `catch` above already proved the class; this proves the tree.
uint $byRoot = 0;
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (Throwable $any) {
    $byRoot = $byRoot + 1;
}
echo "caught by the root of the tree ", $byRoot, " of 1\n";
--EXPECT--
a bare start was refused 1 of 1
and it names the block to write
caught by the root of the tree 1 of 1
